WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.159
Film Courage: When did you read your first
screenwriting book and which one was it?

00:00:03.159 --> 00:00:05.270
Adam William Ward, Actor/Filmmaker: Oh...my
first one?

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:11.639
I think my first book was actually Save The
Cat [Author Blake Snyder], which was a really

00:00:11.639 --> 00:00:14.309
good book and it was about outlining.

00:00:14.309 --> 00:00:18.310
If anybody wants to be a filmmaker, I would
suggest that book (Save The Cat).

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:23.060
If you want to be a filmmaker it lays out
structure really well.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:28.220
Not everything has to be formulaic but almost
everything has been done at this point.

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:35.350
So even when I’m making now where I’m
making this book Catch The White Tiger [Catch

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:36.690
the White Tiger: How I Achieved the American
Dream With $28 by Authors Tony Assali and

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:40.090
Michael Ashley], it’s a biopic so I go and
watch all biopics.

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:42.140
What kind of biopic do I want to make?

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:44.880
THE GULF OF WALL STREET is very different from
THE FLOUNDER, you know what I mean?

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:48.910
So the blueprint is there in a way.

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:55.300
That helps you make a blueprint that book
[Save The Cat].

00:00:55.300 --> 00:01:02.120
That’s a very helpful book but I feel like
watching movies was the most helpful for me.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:05.439
My brother who is my writing partner will
send me script and be like “Here.”

00:01:05.439 --> 00:01:09.230
He just sent me three scripts yesterday of
all of these biopics.

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:14.810
I’d rather watch the movies because I’m
very much...I know that a script can change

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:18.979
so much compared to making a movie.

00:01:18.979 --> 00:01:23.159
When you’re seeing it in the movie it might
be improv, it might be whatever.

00:01:23.159 --> 00:01:24.829
But that could still be a script.

00:01:24.829 --> 00:01:27.850
Maybe it wasn’t a script, maybe it wasn’t
a script but it could have been a script if

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:29.380
you wrote it that way.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:35.399
So I kind of like watching the final product
more than reading the script in the sense

00:01:35.399 --> 00:01:39.810
of learning when it comes to GREATFELLAS reading
the script or watching the movie I’d rather

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:43.450
watch the movie and analyze that.

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:48.840
But my brother is the opposite so it just
depends on how you learn.

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:54.470
And with the Internet you can get your hands
on so many scripts and learn so much.

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:56.780
You can read the script for GREATFELLAS.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:02:01.670
When I was a young kid you could never get
your hands on a script like that unless you

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:05.340
went to the American Film Institute or one
of these big film schools that have those

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:08.410
kind of scripts and then you could read it
and learn from it.

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:11.700
Nowadays people can learn so easily with the
Internet.

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.610
It’s a great thing if you want to learn.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:17.610
Film Courage: What three scripts did he send
you?

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:20.050
Adam: He sent me THE GULF OF WALLSTREET.

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:24.040
He sent me THE FLOUNDER and he sent me GREATFELLAS
(that’s why I brought them all up).

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:28.260
I think I opened THE GULF OF WALLSTREET and read
the first couple of pages and was like “Ahhh.

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:29.500
I’ll watch the movie.”

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:32.270
I don’t know if I’ll read it or not.

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:37.700
But he’s read it and he’s my writing partner.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:41.890
I would like to read them just for fun but
I feel like now is not the time.

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:47.920
For me I get more from a movie than a script
because I’m a filmmaker.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:49.360
He’s a script writer.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:53.430
He wants to just be a script writer (my brother
Charlie Ward).

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:56.290
I want to be a filmmaker and an actor.

00:02:56.290 --> 00:03:00.390
I write scripts out of necessity, I don’t
really want to write scripts.

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:03.440
I write them because I don’t have a script.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:10.550
I’ve been blessed with the fact that at
least I know structure well and I’ve watched...I

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:17.370
used to watch two or three movies a day when
I was a kid and I moved from Philadelphia

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:23.850
when I was a kid to Roseburg, OR and Glide,
OR when I was 12 years old and there was nothing

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:24.850
to do.

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:28.190
I went from the mall and the skating rink
to the like okay there’s a lot of trees

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:29.360
around, there’s nothing to do.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:31.980
The first neighbor is like a mile away.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:37.150
I would just watch movies like crazy, I’d
watch two or three movies a day.

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:40.840
All of my free time was spent watching movies
or running around.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:48.100
I’ve heard it before that you have to spend
10,000 hours on your craft to really be a

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:49.220
master at it.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:57.380
Well I easily watched over 10,000 hours of
movies to be where I’ve gotten.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:03.930
And honestly that’s what makes you an artist
in a way because the things that used to shock

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:09.900
me and impress me and intrigue me as a kid
watching movies are different than what I

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:13.790
do now because I’ve seen so many of them
so it’s different.

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:22.040
You can think back to the time when it did
but ultimately now I have so much more of

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:26.180
a library in my head of movies that now you’re
competing with all of those as to where if

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:31.150
I only saw seven movies this movie is amazing
but it is only competing with six but if you

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:37.670
watch 10,000 hours or whatever now I’ve
seen GODFATHER and GREATFELLAS and they are

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:40.650
both crime movies and now they are competing
against each other.

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:46.180
Some people these days say “This is my favorite
movie.”

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:49.570
But they’ve never seen all of these movies
in the 70’s and 80’s and I’m thinking

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:52.080
but yeah you don’t really know your craft.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:57.520
You have to spend the time to watch all of
those movies and really learn your craft.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:02.900
Film Courage: Would you say that Charlie is
good at one part of screenwriting and you’re

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:03.900
good at the other?

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:06.430
I mean how do you work together with a partner?

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:07.660
How is that?

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:09.310
Adam: With Seth it was different.

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:11.130
So Seth Hymes and me wrote WALLY GOT WASTED.

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:14.090
Charle is writing this new movie with me.

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:15.980
two very different writing partners for me.

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:20.260
Seth really wanted to put the words on the
page.

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:26.540
And so I would basically do the outline and
he would go off and write by himself and then

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:29.940
come bring it back to me and then I’d read
it and make all of these corrections on the

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:33.380
page and be like this scene doesn’t hit
this moment.

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:34.590
I need this moment.

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:40.300
This is like...(as an example) this is the
low of the movie.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:41.660
These guys have to turn on each other.

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:47.110
I need a good monologue here of him turning
on him and no hope you know.”

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:50.901
And that was one of the scenes that Seth couldn’t
end up doing on is own but most of them I’d

00:05:50.901 --> 00:05:57.160
give different notes and he would go and make
funny moments and do certain things that he

00:05:57.160 --> 00:05:59.320
would change and write it.

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:03.610
And then the low of the movie in WALLY GOT
WASTED there on a cliffside overlooking all

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:06.889
of Los Angeles he could never get that scene
so I was like “Okay, I’ll write it.”

00:06:06.889 --> 00:06:11.100
So then I wrote that scene and the joke is
in her mind but the jokes and the other scene

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:12.480
are his.

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:14.530
It’s nice to write in a group.

00:06:14.530 --> 00:06:19.690
ATTACHMENT THEORY I wrote on my own by myself.

00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:24.080
But WALLY GOT WASTED was with Seth and then
this new one is with my brother.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:28.790
My brother and me have been writing scripts
since I was a child so we have very much shorthand

00:06:28.790 --> 00:06:39.210
with each other of thinking a lot of the same
things and stuff like that.

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:43.110
Funny I have not found anybody that we like
to write words on the page together.

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:47.930
It’s kind of like he goes off and writes,
I go off and write, and we come together.

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:53.630
I’ve yet to find somebody who likes to write
in the room together but that would be interesting.

00:06:53.630 --> 00:06:55.110
It would be a dream come true in a way.

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:56.110
It would be fun.

00:06:56.110 --> 00:06:58.840
And I’ve done that with my brother.

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:01.700
PAROLE OFFICERS we did together.

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:05.990
I acted out all of the characters in the living
room and he sat there and would type.

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:09.790
So I’m like living the characters and saying
what they would say and then he’s like writing

00:07:09.790 --> 00:07:10.790
them down.

00:07:10.790 --> 00:07:15.320
It was a very different process with PAROLE
OFFICERS.

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:17.540
But most of them had people who wanted to
write alone.

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:21.930
They want their creativity to be quiet and
all of that kind of stuff so I was just different.

